## Unshadow

I used:

```bash
unshadow local_passwd local_shadow > unshadowed.txt
```

Then checked the generated file:

```bash
cat unshadowed.txt
```

The idea was:

```text
local_passwd
     +
local_shadow
     ↓
unshadow
     ↓
unshadowed.txt
```

---

## Cracking

I used:

```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt --format=sha512crypt unshadowed.txt
```

I also tried:

```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt unshadowed.txt
```

The first command explicitly specified the format.

---

## Mistakes while experimenting

I tried:

```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt unshadowed.txt --shadow
```

and:

```bash
john --shadow unshadowed.txt
```

These were incorrect experiments because `--shadow` was not the option needed for displaying the cracked password from the generated `unshadowed.txt`.

The correct display option was:

```bash
john --show unshadowed.txt
```

### Important difference

```text
--show
↓
show already cracked passwords

--shadow
↓
not the option I needed for this task
```

I also tried:

```bash
john --show /home/user/src/john/run/john.pot
```

This was another misunderstanding.

`john.pot` is John's password-result storage file; it is not normally the input file you give to `--show`.

The useful form is:

```bash
john --show unshadowed.txt
```

