# Task 09 — ZIP Cracking

I entered:

```bash
cd ~/John-the-Ripper-The-Basics/Task09
```

Then checked:

```bash
ls
```

I discovered `zip2john`.

---

## My incorrect attempts

I initially experimented with:

```bash
zip2john -o secure.zip > hash.txt
```

```bash
zip2john -s secure.zip > hash.txt
```

```bash
zip2john -c secure.zip > hash.txt
```

These were unnecessary because the basic command already did what I needed.

The correct command was:

```bash
zip2john secure.zip > hash.txt
```

Then:

```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hash.txt
```

And:

```bash
john --show hash.txt
```

---

## Mistake: Giving the ZIP directly to John

I tried:

```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt secure.zip
```

### Why it was wrong

John needs the ZIP converted into a John-readable hash first.

Correct flow:

```text
secure.zip
   ↓
zip2john
   ↓
ziphash.txt
   ↓
john
```

Not:

```text
secure.zip
   ↓
john
```

---

## Mistake: `--worlist`

I tried:

```bash
john --worlist=usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt secure.zip
```

### Why it was wrong

The option is:

```bash
--wordlist=
```

not:

```bash
--worlist=
```

I also omitted the `/` before `usr` in that attempt.

Correct:

```bash
--wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt
```

---

## Extracting the ZIP

After cracking the password, I used:

```bash
unzip secure.zip
```

Then:

```bash
ls
cd zippy
ls
cat flag.txt
```

This confirmed that cracking the password and extracting the archive are two separate steps.

